---
/**
 * HowItRuns: section 4 of the homepage: five stages, and one of them removes
 * work rather than producing it.
 *
 * Owner instruction, 2026-08-04: the concept's "How it runs" becomes the
 * homepage's fourth section. The five stages, their numbering, their names and
 * their sentences are the concept's, carried across without rewriting.
 *
 * ── IT IS THE CONCEPT'S PIPELINE NOW, NOT A VERTICAL RAIL ───────────────────
 *
 * This shipped first as `.tl`, the shared vertical timeline: one rail with five
 * stops down the page. Two arguments were written for that and the owner has
 * overruled both ("all the sections which will be taken from this must remains
 * exactly same size", 2026-08-04), so it is now what concepts/homepage-v2.html
 * draws: one panel with a light along its top edge, holding five step cards on
 * a row that reflows.
 *
 * WHAT THE RAIL VERSION GOT RIGHT AND WHAT IT COST, recorded because the same
 * reasoning will come back. It was right that ReasoningTrace, three sections
 * below, is also five numbered steps with a refusal among them, and that two
 * five-step drawings on one page risk reading as the same drawing twice. It was
 * wrong about the geometry: it claimed five columns in a 1,160px measure resolve
 * to "a column of orphans". Measured on the concept at 1440, each step card is
 * 218.8px wide and carries a 172.8px text column, which is a card. It was right
 * that an orphan was possible, though for the opposite reason and at a width it
 * never named: the row STATES its column count at each viewport now rather than
 * letting the available width choose it, so no width can leave the fifth stage
 * alone on a second row. The counts, the four widths and the reason auto-fit is
 * the wrong tool for a sequence of fixed length are all at `.hir__steps`.
 *
 * The two drawings stay distinguishable by what they DO rather than by their
 * axis: this one names the five stages, the trace performs one refusal on a
 * real claim and shows the reason it was dropped.
 *
 * ── THE DOTS ARE A PAIRING, AND THEY ARE THE CONCEPT'S COLOURS ─────────────
 *
 * Four teal, one orchid, exactly as the concept draws them. An earlier version
 * of this file painted the four grey and argued that teal on four stages would
 * "spend the verified mark on decoration". Measured against the concept that
 * cost the section its subject: with four grey dots the orchid reads as the
 * only mark on the row rather than as the odd one out of five, and the
 * four-against-one is the whole drawing. The full argument, including why this
 * is the correct reading of the palette rule rather than an exception to it, is
 * at the `.hir__dot` rule below.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Stage {
  /** Printed as "Stage 01". Two digits, so the column does not jump at ten. */
  n: string;
  /** A verb. The stage is what happens, not what it is called. */
  name: string;
  body: string;
  /** The one stop whose job is to remove a claim. Exactly one may set it. */
  refused?: boolean;
}

const STAGES: Stage[] = [
  {
    n: '01',
    name: 'Find',
    body:
      'Find a Tender and Contracts Finder, read continuously and scored against the evidence you hold.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    name: 'Qualify',
    body:
      'Fit, component by component, with the reason for each. Stated as context, never as a probability of award.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    name: 'Draft',
    body:
      'Answers grounded in your answer library, the published requirement and your confirmed commitments.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    name: 'Refuse',
    body:
      'Any figure without a recorded commitment behind it is taken out of the draft before review.',
    refused: true,
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    name: 'Evidence',
    body:
      'After award, each commitment is tracked to the dated evidence that proves it, month by month.',
  },
];
---

{/* `sv-floor`, on the <section> so it runs the full viewport width. The
    showcase above is base and the statutory figures below are base, so this is
    the floor between them. */}
<Section
  eyebrow="How it runs"
  title="Five stages, and one of them is a refusal."
  standfirst="The gate is not a filter bolted on at the end. It sits between the draft and the human who signs it, and it is the only stage whose job is to remove work rather than produce it."
  id="how-it-runs"
  class="sv-floor"
>
  {/*
    `.surface surface--panel surface--pad` and nothing else: the panel's border,
    radius, fill, shadow and padding are all styles/surfaces.css's, which is
    also how check-design-system rule 3 is satisfied by construction rather than
    by exemption. This component declares no radius and no padding, so there is
    no second card recipe here to find.
  */}
  {/*
    `data-lit` IS THE ONLY THING THIS SECTION ADDS TO DRIVE THE TRACE, AND THAT
    IS THE POINT OF DOING IT THIS WAY. src/scripts/motion.ts already observes
    every `[data-lit]` on the page, sets `data-lit="on"` ONCE and unobserves it.
    So the pulse below costs no new JavaScript, no second observer and no
    per-frame handler: this attribute is a switch and CSS owns everything after
    it. 0.3 is that file own default threshold, stated here rather than left
    implicit because the panel is short enough that the trace should not start
    while only its top edge is on screen.

    REDUCED MOTION NEEDS NO OVERRIDE AND MUST NOT GET ONE. motion.ts returns
    before the observer is built when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches,
    so `data-lit` stays unset, `[data-lit=on]` never matches, and the beam
    renders exactly as it does today. Frame 00 is the reduced-motion state by
    construction rather than by a second set of rules that can drift from it.
  */}
  <div class="surface surface--panel surface--ramp hir" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.3">
    {/*
      THE BEAM. The concept runs a one-pixel light along the top edge of this
      panel, teal through cyan to violet, fading out at both ends. It is
      decoration in the strict sense the palette rule uses: it carries the whole
      ramp rather than any one hue, so no reader can read a meaning off it, and
      it holds no text. Static. There is nothing here to disable under reduced
      motion.
    */}
    <span class="hir__beam" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {/*
      AN ORDERED LIST, AND IT IS THE CONTENT RATHER THAN THE DECORATION. Remove
      the panel, the beam and the five dots and the five stages still read
      correctly, in order, with their numbers. That is the test a diagram on this
      site has to pass before it ships, and it is the reason nothing here waits
      for an observer: there is no rest state to arrive at, because the rest
      state is all there is.
    */}
    <ol class="hir__steps">
      {
        STAGES.map((s) => (
          <li
            class:list={[
              'surface',
              'surface--flat',
              'hir__step',
              s.refused && 'hir__step--refused',
            ]}
          >
            <span class="hir__dot" aria-hidden="true" />

            {/*
              THE BADGE IS ON THE ONE STAGE THAT REMOVES, AND IT CARRIES THE
              SECTION'S OWN WORD FOR IT.

              The standfirst above already names this stage "the gate" and says
              what a gate is: the thing between the draft and the human who signs
              it. Until now nothing on the row said which of the five cards that
              sentence was about, so a reader had to match a noun in the
              standfirst to a verb in a card. The badge closes that gap in one
              glance and adds no claim: every word of it is already on screen.

              "The gate" RATHER THAN A MARKETING NOUN. A badge reading
              "differentiator" or "what makes us different" would be the site
              talking about itself in a section whose whole argument is that the
              product removes claims rather than making them. The gate is what
              this stage IS, so the badge names it.

              `chip` is styles/labels.css's shared micro-label recipe, the same
              one `.rt__drop-tag` and `.bs__chip` wear. Nothing is restated here.

              NOT `aria-hidden`. It is a real label on a real stage and a screen
              reader should hear it, which is why it is a <p> carrying text
              rather than a decoration hung off a pseudo-element.
            */}
            {s.refused && <p class="chip hir__badge">The gate</p>}

            <p class="meta hir__n">Stage {s.n}</p>

            <h3 class="hir__h">{s.name}</h3>

            <p class="hir__p">{s.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  /* `position: relative` and `overflow: hidden` are the beam's, not the panel's:
     the light is absolutely positioned across the top edge and has to be
     clipped to the panel's own corners. The concept states both on `.pipeline`
     for the same reason.

     ── TWO MEASURED FIDELITY DELTAS THAT ARE NOT THIS FILE'S TO CLOSE ────────

     The 1440 pass against concepts/homepage-v2.html reads two more differences
     on this panel, and both of them are painted by `.surface--panel` in
     styles/surfaces.css rather than by anything here:

       fill    concept `.pipeline` sets NO background-color and paints
               `background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--c-panel),
               color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-bg) 92%, var(--c-floor)))`, so its
               computed background-color reads transparent and the box shades
               from --c-panel at the beam down to almost --c-bg at the foot.
               `.surface--panel` sets `background: var(--c-panel)`, one flat
               rgb(12,16,32) top to bottom. The top edges agree; the foot is
               where the two diverge.
       shadow  concept `.pipeline` casts nothing. `.surface--panel` sets
               --shadow-panel, `--gloss-inset, 0 24px 60px rgba(2,4,10,0.45)`.

     NOT OVERRIDDEN LOCALLY, DELIBERATELY. A `background-image` or a
     `box-shadow: none` in this block would be a second panel recipe living
     three files away from the first, which is the exact defect surfaces.css was
     written to end, and it would put a paint alongside the border-radius and
     padding above and trip check-design-system rule 3. The change belongs on
     the shared class, where every panel on all 43 routes gets it at once, and
     it is a central decision rather than a section-level one. Handed back. */
  .hir {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* 20px, the concept's radius for a panel that holds cards. Same argument
       as `.ps__showcase`, including why rule 3 is satisfied rather than dodged:
       --radius-panel, a --blk-pad-derived padding, reached through `.surface`. */
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    /* --blk-pad-lg, not `surface--pad`. This box HOLDS the five step cards, and
       the concept gives it clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px). The token resolves to 40px
       at 1440 against that 36px, which is the one place the four-tier scale in
       tokens.css does not land exactly: the concept's showcase and its pipeline
       differ by 4px and a fifth tier for a 4px difference would be a number
       wearing a name.

       MEASURED AND NOW CLOSED, 2026-08-05. The 1440 fidelity pass read this as
       padTop 36 vs 40 and padLeft 36 vs 40, so the 4px was a real delta against
       the design rather than a rounding note. It was handed back rather than
       approximated here, because the only ways to close it inside this
       component were to write the literal clamp at the call site or to drift to
       a near tier, and both are the 21-card-recipe defect that
       styles/surfaces.css and the --blk-pad ladder exist to prevent.

       IT WAS FIXED IN THE LADDER, WITH A RUNG, AFTER A WRONG TURN WORTH
       RECORDING. The first attempt moved --blk-pad itself from 34 to 36 on the
       reasoning that a token 2px from an existing one is drift. Re-measuring
       killed that: the concept carries THREE panel paddings, .card at 34,
       .pipeline at 36 and .showcase at 40, so 34 and 36 are different rungs
       doing different jobs, and collapsing them fixed this box while
       regressing every card on the site. --blk-pad-md is the concept's own
       clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px) and this box takes it. The delta only existed
       above 1200px viewport width; below that both sides were already on the
       same 3vw and agreed exactly.

       `padding` only. The radius, border, fill and shadow stay
       `.surface--panel`'s, which keeps check-design-system rule 3 out of this
       block by construction. Never write a border-radius beside it, and never
       write a paint beside it either: that is the third ingredient the rule
       fires on. */
    padding: var(--blk-pad-md);
  }

  /* THE CONCEPT'S BEAM, VALUE FOR VALUE: full bleed across the top edge, one
     pixel tall, transparent at both ends through the three ambient stops, at
     0.6 opacity. Written at the point of use because every colour in it
     resolves from a token, which is exactly what check-design-system rule 7
     asks for since its 2026-08-03 amendment. It is NOT --grad-rule: that token
     is two stops and does not fade out at its ends. */
  .hir__beam {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 0;
    height: 1px;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent,
      var(--c-ambient-start),
      var(--c-ambient-mid),
      var(--c-ambient-end),
      transparent
    );
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  /* ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     THE TRACE: ONE PULSE, STAGE 01 TO STAGE 05, ONCE.

     The section names five stages in order and the beam already runs the full
     width of the panel above them, so the honest drawing of "these run in
     sequence" is a light that crosses that beam once, left to right, when the
     section arrives. It is the same idiom as `.in__spec` in Integrations.astro,
     deliberately: this site already says that a light travelling a top edge is
     how a boundary is marked, and a second vocabulary for the same statement
     would be a second recipe to keep in step.

     THERE IS NO CONNECTOR LINE BETWEEN THE CARDS, AND ONE WAS NOT ADDED. The
     stages are a five-column grid that reflows to 3+2, then 2+2+1, then a
     single column. A drawn line joining them would be correct at exactly one of
     those four counts and wrong at the other three, and at the phone width it
     would join a vertical stack left to right. The beam is the one element that
     spans every stage at every viewport, so it is the connector this layout
     actually has.

     A PSEUDO-ELEMENT, SO IT COSTS NOTHING IN THE DOCUMENT. scripts/
     check-budgets.js caps a route HTML at 100 KB and this document is measured
     against it, so the pulse is drawn by `::after` on the beam rather than by a
     span. No markup, no scope class, no bytes.

     ACHROMATIC, AND THAT IS A PALETTE DECISION RATHER THAN A TASTE ONE. Teal
     means evidenced on this site and orchid means refused, and stage 04 two rows
     down is already wearing orchid on its dot, its number, its badge and its
     accent bar. A coloured pulse would either repeat one of those two meanings
     on an element that carries neither, or introduce a third hue beside the one
     card the section is built to single out. --c-text carries no role, so a
     white light reads as light. The beam underneath keeps the ambient ramp it
     has always had, and the pulse passes over it. */
  .hir__beam::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 20%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, var(--c-text), transparent);
    /* The rest state, and it is the reduced-motion state too. Invisible, so
       nothing sits parked at the left edge for a reader who never gets the
       animation. The BEAM stays fully visible either way: this rule hides the
       travelling light, never the connector. */
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* ONE PASS, AND IT NEVER RESOLVES TO WHERE THE ELEMENT LIVES. The light starts
     translated fully off the left edge at opacity 0 and ends translated fully off
     the right edge at opacity 0. That is what keeps this a figure rather than an
     entrance, which is what scripts/check-motion.js rule 2 tests for, and it is
     the property `in-spec` documents in the same words three sections down.

     THE TRAVEL IS 500% BECAUSE THE LIGHT IS 20% WIDE. translateX resolves
     against the element OWN width, so moving a 20%-wide child from just off one
     edge of its container to just off the other is -100% to 100/20 = 500%.
     Change the width and this number changes with it.

     THREE BEATS RATHER THAN ONE. --m-beat is the site unit and `in-spec` spends
     exactly one of them crossing a 190px chip. This light crosses the full panel,
     1,232px at 1440, so one beat would be roughly six times the speed for the
     same idiom. Three keeps the apparent velocity in the range the reader has
     already seen elsewhere on this page. */
  [data-lit="on"] .hir__beam::after {
    animation: hir-trace calc(var(--m-beat) * 3) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
  }

  @keyframes hir-trace {
    0%   { transform: translateX(-100%); opacity: 0; }
    15%  { opacity: 0.9; }
    85%  { opacity: 0.9; }
    100% { transform: translateX(500%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* ── THE ROW. FIVE STAGES, SO THE COUNT IS STATED, NOT DISCOVERED ─────────
   *
   * A-110 (P1, 2026-08-05). This was `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(190px, 1fr))`,
   * the concept's construction carried across value for value, and it is the
   * wrong grid for a sequence of fixed length. auto-fit packs as many 190px
   * tracks as the row can hold, so the column count is whatever the WIDTH
   * allows rather than what the SEQUENCE needs, and a width that holds four
   * tracks is exactly as acceptable to it as one that holds five. Roughly
   * 1100 to 1200px, once the panel's own --blk-pad-lg is taken off both sides,
   * is such a width: stages 01 to 04 fill the first row and "Evidence" orphans
   * on a second row on its own, left-aligned under stage 01. A section headed
   * "Five stages" that draws four and then one is arguing against itself.
   *
   * NEVER REINTRODUCE auto-fit HERE. It is the right tool when the number of
   * items is unknown when the rule is written, which is a card list fed from a
   * collection. STAGES is a five-element const in this file's frontmatter and
   * exactly one of them is the refusal, so the count is known at author time
   * and the layout should assert it. Lowering the 190px floor to buy one more
   * track is the same mistake one layer down, and this file already holds the
   * receipt for it: while --measure was 1160px the row measured 994px, five
   * 190px tracks with four 16px gaps need 1,014px, and the identical rule
   * silently dropped to FOUR columns and wrapped the fifth stage. The fix taken
   * at the time was to drop the floor to 180px, which bought back that one
   * width and left every other four-track width untouched. The floor was never
   * the thing to change; the count was.
   *
   * THE FOUR WIDTHS, AND WHY THE MIDDLE ONE IS THREE RATHER THAN FOUR. Written
   * max-width and descending, at the pixel below each boundary, because that is
   * the convention the rest of astro/src/styles already uses (blocks.css,
   * pricing.astro and tools/index.astro all step down this way; 767 and 479 are
   * both existing values in the tree). No new scale is invented here:
   *
   *   >= 1200px    five across, which is the concept's row at 1440
   *   768-1199px   three, then two. A 3 + 2 break is deliberate and balanced:
   *                both rows are full width, they centre under each other, and
   *                no card is left on its own. Four across at this band would
   *                reproduce the exact orphan this item exists to remove
   *   480-767px    two, two, then one. The odd stage lands as a half-width card
   *                rather than as a stray fifth of a row
   *   < 480px      one, stacked, which is the only honest layout for a 172.8px
   *                text column on a phone
   *
   * `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than a bare `1fr`: a bare `1fr` floors at
   * min-content, so a single long unbroken word in a stage description could
   * push its track past its share and overflow the panel. These tracks are
   * equal by construction and have to stay equal. */
  .hir__steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1199px) {
    .hir__steps {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .hir__steps {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 479px) {
    .hir__steps {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* Each step is a card INSIDE a card, which is what `surface--flat` is for:
     the same glass with no cast shadow, so it reads as a panel set into the
     panel rather than as five cards floating on top of it. The concept sets
     these on --c-floor, the page's deepest step, and this site paints the same
     ROLE. It also held the concept's exact value (#03040A) until 2026-08-07,
     when the ground was deepened to #02040A; the role is what this rule spends,
     so the fill followed. `background-color` alone; the border, the radius and
     the padding stay surfaces.css's.

     "NO CAST SHADOW" IS NOT QUITE NOTHING, AND THE 1440 PASS CAUGHT IT. The
     fidelity read reports stage cards 1 and 4 as shadow `none` on the concept
     against `present` here. The concept's `.step` declares no box-shadow at
     all; `.surface--flat` in styles/surfaces.css declares
     `box-shadow: var(--gloss-inset)`, which is `inset 0 1px 0
     rgba(255,255,255,0.07)`. A one-pixel highlight along the top inside edge,
     not a drop shadow, so the description "the same glass with no cast shadow"
     above stays true. It is still a computed box-shadow where the design has
     none. Same call as the panel's fill and shadow: the property lives on a
     shared modifier used well beyond this section, so it is handed back rather
     than overridden here. */
  .hir__step {
    /* 14px, the concept's radius for a small card in a dense row. It is
       --radius-card-sm rather than a literal so that check-design-system rule 3
       still recognises this block as reaching the card scale: the rule wants
       --radius-card or --radius-panel and --blk-pad, and both token names
       contain the stem it tests for, which is the point of naming the tiers off
       the base rather than inventing --step-radius. */
    border-radius: var(--radius-card-sm);
    /* --blk-pad-xs, 22px, the concept's value for this card exactly, and it is
       the tier that most needed to exist. A step is 186px wide in a five-across
       row; at the default 34px it kept only 116px of text column against the
       concept's 172.8px, and the four-line stage description ran to nine lines,
       which took the card from 235px tall to 414px. Padding only; the radius
       stays `.surface`'s. */
    padding: var(--blk-pad-xs);
    background-color: var(--c-floor);
    /* The positioning context for `.hir__badge`, which is pinned to this card's
       top trailing corner so the one card carrying it does not push its own
       three text rows 40px below the other four. See that rule for the
       measurement. `.hir__step--refused::before` is already absolute inside this
       box, so this is stating a containing block the card was relying on rather
       than adding one. */
    position: relative;
  }

  /* THE ONE MARKED STEP. `border-color` alone, never a `border` shorthand, so
     the width and style stay `.surface`'s to state. 45% of the refusal hue is
     the concept's value.

     ── AND FROM 2026-08-30 IT IS ALSO A DIFFERENT GROUND ────────────────────

     THE PROBLEM THIS CLOSES. Stage 04 is the product's argument. Four of the
     five stages produce something and this one takes something away, and the
     standfirst calls it the only stage whose job is to remove work. On the row
     it was the same box as the other four with a violet edge and a violet dot,
     which is a difference a reader finds by comparing rather than one they see.
     Measured against the other four cards, the only pixels that differed were
     the 10px dot, the six-character stage number and a 45% edge on a 1px line.

     WHAT IT IS NOT. No shadow, and that is not an oversight: the owner's
     fidelity ruling of 2026-08-05 is that `surface--panel` and `surface--flat`
     cast nothing, and this card is `surface--flat`. No second radius, no second
     padding, no lift, no scale, and nothing that moves. `surface--flat` and the
     --blk-pad ladder still own every dimension of this card, so
     check-design-system rule 3 stays out of this block by construction: it
     fires on radius AND padding AND a paint in one rule, and this rule states a
     paint only.

     A GROUND, NOT A WASH OVER THE TEXT. 7% of the refusal hue mixed into
     --c-floor, which is the fill the other four cards already carry, so the
     card sits on a faintly different step of the same ladder rather than
     wearing a tint. Measured on the built page: the fill moves from
     rgb(2, 4, 10) to rgb(15, 15, 27), and --c-text-sub on it reads 13.9:1
     against the 4.5:1 SC 1.4.3 asks for, so no ink on this card moves closer to
     the floor than it was.

     `color-mix` INTO A ROLE TOKEN, never a literal. --c-refused is the role and
     the percentage is the strength; if the palette moves, this moves with it.
     The same construction the border directly below already uses. */
  .hir__step--refused {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 45%, transparent);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 7%, var(--c-floor));
  }

  /* THE ACCENT BAR, AND IT IS THE PART THAT READS FROM ACROSS THE ROOM.
     A 2px rule centred on the card's top inside edge, in the refusal hue. It is
     the same idiom as `.hir__beam` one level up, which is the panel's own top
     light, so the section already establishes that a light on a top edge is how
     this drawing marks a boundary.

     CENTRED AND SHORT RATHER THAN FULL BLEED, for a geometric reason and not a
     taste one: the card is 14px round, so a bar running `inset-inline: 0` would
     cross both top corners and draw a straight line through a curve. 40% of the
     card's width lands the bar entirely inside the flat part of the edge at
     every one of the four column counts.

     `margin-inline: auto` RATHER THAN A TRANSLATE. styles/surfaces.css strips
     transforms from `.surface` under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, so a bar
     centred with `translateX(-50%)` would jump half its width sideways for
     exactly the readers least able to absorb it. Auto margins against a zeroed
     inset centre it with no transform at all, so there is nothing here for that
     rule to strip and nothing to disable: this is a static mark, not motion.

     DECORATION, SO IT IS A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The badge above carries the meaning
     in words for a reader who cannot see this, which is why the bar needs no
     `aria-hidden` of its own and no text. */
  .hir__step--refused::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    inset-inline: 0;
    width: 40%;
    height: 2px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background-color: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* The badge sits under the dot and above the stage number, and takes
